1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,712
Welcome to Bangside Revolution.

2
00:00:02,662 --> 00:00:06,025
This lesson is called Build Your First Webpage.

3
00:00:06,025 --> 00:00:12,025
Today, you will learn the foundations of web development by creating a simple personal portfolio.

4
00:00:12,025 --> 00:00:14,962
You do not need to be an expert programmer.

5
00:00:14,962 --> 00:00:20,450
You need a device, a text editor, a browser, and the willingness to experiment.

6
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:25,475
Let's start with two essential building blocks: HTML and CSS.

7
00:00:25,475 --> 00:00:29,275
HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language.

8
00:00:29,275 --> 00:00:32,950
It defines the structure and content of a webpage.

9
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:38,937
Headings, paragraphs, links, and lists are all examples of HTML content.

10
00:00:38,937 --> 00:00:42,362
CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets.

11
00:00:42,362 --> 00:00:49,075
CSS controls presentation: colors, spacing, fonts, borders, and page layout.

12
00:00:49,075 --> 00:00:54,837
Think of HTML as the structure of a building and CSS as the design of its rooms.

13
00:00:54,837 --> 00:00:58,412
They work together, but they serve different purposes.

14
00:00:58,412 --> 00:01:01,925
You do not have to memorize every tag or property.

15
00:01:01,925 --> 00:01:06,750
Learn to read the code, make one small change, and observe what happens.

16
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:09,750
Part one: understand the structure.

17
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:16,400
A basic HTML document begins with a declaration that tells the browser to use modern HTML.

18
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:19,812
The HTML element contains the page.

19
00:01:19,812 --> 00:01:24,450
The head contains information about the document, including its title.

20
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:27,562
The body contains the content people see.

21
00:01:27,562 --> 00:01:30,075
Headings organize information.

22
00:01:30,075 --> 00:01:32,650
Paragraphs explain ideas.

23
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:35,537
Unordered lists group related items.

24
00:01:35,537 --> 00:01:40,237
Opening and closing tags usually work together, so keep them paired.

25
00:01:40,237 --> 00:01:43,387
Part two: create your first page.

26
00:01:43,387 --> 00:01:48,275
Open a text editor and create a new file named index dot html.

27
00:01:48,275 --> 00:01:51,725
Add a heading introducing yourself or your project.

28
00:01:51,725 --> 00:01:56,475
Under it, write a short paragraph explaining what you are learning or building.

29
00:01:56,475 --> 00:01:59,800
Then add a list of three skills you are developing.

30
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:01,662
Save the file.

31
00:02:01,662 --> 00:02:03,900
Open it in a web browser.

32
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:07,862
You should see your heading, introduction, and skills list.

33
00:02:07,862 --> 00:02:11,625
If something does not appear correctly, do not panic.

34
00:02:11,625 --> 00:02:19,825
Check the spelling of your tags, make sure the closing tags are present, and confirm that you saved the file with the HTML extension.

35
00:02:19,825 --> 00:02:22,437
Part three: make it your own.

36
00:02:22,437 --> 00:02:27,200
Use the portfolio generator on the lesson page to create a starter file.

37
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:30,462
Choose a public display name or a project name.

38
00:02:30,462 --> 00:02:32,600
Write a short tagline.

39
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:37,787
Add skills you are genuinely developing, and describe a project you are working on.

40
00:02:37,787 --> 00:02:39,912
Protect your privacy.

41
00:02:39,912 --> 00:02:47,337
Do not put your home address, passwords, private account details, or other sensitive information into a portfolio.

42
00:02:47,337 --> 00:02:51,887
Before publishing anything, review what visitors will be able to see.

43
00:02:51,887 --> 00:02:54,662
Part four: test your work.

44
00:02:54,662 --> 00:03:01,050
Download your generated HTML file and open it with a browser or compatible HTML editor.

45
00:03:01,050 --> 00:03:05,737
Confirm that the heading, introduction, skills, and project appear.

46
00:03:05,737 --> 00:03:08,537
Now change one sentence in the code.

47
00:03:08,537 --> 00:03:11,062
Save the file and reopen it.

48
00:03:11,062 --> 00:03:13,637
Confirm that your change appears.

49
00:03:13,637 --> 00:03:19,137
Keep a copy of the original and your edited version so you can recover if something goes wrong.

50
00:03:19,137 --> 00:03:23,650
Remember: a webpage saved on your device is a local project.

51
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:26,325
It is not yet a public website.

52
00:03:26,325 --> 00:03:32,925
Publishing requires a hosting service or web server, and you should review your content before making it public.

53
00:03:32,925 --> 00:03:35,475
Here is your completion challenge.

54
00:03:35,475 --> 00:03:40,975
Explain, in your own words, what HTML does and what CSS does.

55
00:03:40,975 --> 00:03:43,700
Generate your portfolio page.

56
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:45,862
Open it successfully.

57
00:03:45,862 --> 00:03:49,487
Then make one change yourself and verify the result.

58
00:03:49,487 --> 00:03:55,387
You have completed this lesson when you can perform those steps without simply copying instructions.

59
00:03:55,387 --> 00:04:01,925
Technology becomes more useful when you can understand it, shape it, and use it to build something of your own.

60
00:04:01,925 --> 00:04:07,075
Start small, test your work, protect your information, and keep learning.

61
00:04:07,075 --> 00:04:10,175
Thank you for learning with Bangside Revolution.

62
00:04:10,175 --> 00:04:17,687
Your next step is to keep improving your portfolio and explore how a local webpage can eventually become a published website.
